  "raw_output": "{\n  \"exercise_type\": \"CEFR English Placement Test\",\n  \"section_id\": 5,\n  \"section_title\": \"Reading Comprehension\",\n  \"cefr_range\": \"B2-C1\",\n  \"reading_text\": \"The concept of time is a complex and multifaceted one, influenced by various cultural, social, and personal factors. In many non-Western cultures, time is viewed as cyclical rather than linear, with events recurring in cycles of birth, growth, decay, and rebirth. This perspective is often reflected in the use of natural imagery to describe temporal relationships. For example, the Chinese concept of the 'wheel of life' represents the cyclical nature of existence, while the Hindu idea of karma emphasizes the interconnectedness of past, present, and future actions.\n\nIn contrast, Western cultures tend to view time as a linear progression, with events unfolding in a straight line from past to present to future. This perspective is often reflected in the use of clock-like imagery to describe temporal relationships. For instance, the phrase 'time is money' suggests that time is a valuable resource to be spent or wasted, while the concept of 'lost time' implies a sense of regret and missed opportunity.\n\nDespite these differences, both Eastern and Western cultures share a common concern with the passage of time and its impact on human experience. Whether viewed as cyclical or linear, time remains a fundamental aspect of our lives, shaping our perceptions, behaviors, and understanding of the world around us.\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 36,\n      \"question\": \"What is the main idea of the passage?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Time is viewed differently in Eastern and Western cultures.\", \"The concept of time is influenced by cultural, social, and personal factors.\", \"The passage of time has a significant impact on human experience.\", \"The wheel of life is a concept unique to Chinese culture.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"The concept of time is influenced by cultural, social, and personal factors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 37,\n      \"question\": \"According to the passage, what is the main difference between Eastern and Western cultures' views on time?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Eastern cultures view time as cyclical, while Western cultures view it as linear.\", \"Western cultures tend to focus on the past, while Eastern cultures focus on the future.\", \"The wheel of life is a concept unique to Western culture.\", \"Time is viewed as a valuable resource in both Eastern and Western cultures.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"Eastern cultures view time as cyclical, while Western cultures view it as linear.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 38,\n      \"question\": \"What does the phrase 'time is money' suggest about the Western perspective on time?\",\n      \"options\": [\"That time is a valuable resource to be spent or wasted.\", \"That time is a fixed quantity that cannot be changed.\", \"That time is a flexible concept that can be manipulated at will.\", \"That time has no significance in human experience.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"That time is a valuable resource to be spent or wasted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 39,\n      \"question\": \"What inference can be made about the author's attitude towards the concept of time?\",\n      \"options\": [\"The author views time as a linear progression.\", \"The author believes that time has no significance in human experience.\", \"The author is concerned with the impact of time on human experience.\", \"The author thinks that time is a fixed quantity that cannot be changed.\"],\n      \"answer\": \"The author is concerned with the impact of time on human experience.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 40,\n      \"question\": \"What does the passage suggest about the relationship between past, present, and future actions?\",\n      \"options\": [\"That they are mutually exclusive.\", \"That they are interconnected and 